message:
Running command: # Let me check if the original testcase crashes with a 512-byte buffer
# (which is what KTar uses for readData)
cat > /workspace/test_ktar.c << 'EOF'
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <zlib.h>

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    if (argc < 2) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s <gzip_file>\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    FILE *f = fopen(argv[1], "rb");
    if (!f) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Cannot open %s\n", argv[1]);
        return 1;
    }

    fseek(f, 0, SEEK_END);
    long fsize = ftell(f);
    fseek(f, 0, SEEK_SET);

    unsigned char *inbuf = malloc(fsize);
    fread(inbuf, 1, fsize, f);
    fclose(f);

    z_stream strm;
    memset(&strm, 0, sizeof(strm));

    int ret = inflateInit2(&strm, MAX_WBITS + 32);
    if (ret != Z_OK) {
        fprintf(stderr, "inflateInit2 failed: %d\n", ret);
        return 1;
    }

    strm.next_in = inbuf;
    strm.avail_in = fsize;

    // Simulate KTar: read 512 bytes at a time
    unsigned char outbuf[512];
    unsigned char *next_out = outbuf;
    unsigned avail_out = 512;

    strm.next_out = next_out;
    strm.avail_out = avail_out;

    ret = inflate(&strm, Z_SYNC_FLUSH);
    printf("inflate returned: %d\n", ret);
    printf("avail_in: %u, avail_out: %u\n", strm.avail_in, strm.avail_out);
    printf("total_out: %lu\n", strm.total_out);
    printf("msg: %s\n", strm.msg ? strm.msg : "NULL");

    // Check for overflow
    for (int i = 512; i < 1024; i++) {
        if (outbuf[i] != 0) {
            printf("OVERFLOW at offset %d: 0x%02x\n", i, outbuf[i]);
        }
    }

    inflateEnd(&strm);
    free(inbuf);
    return 0;
}
EOF
cd /workspace && gcc -fsanitize=address -g -o test_ktar_asan test_ktar.c -I /workspace/repo-vul/src-vul/zlib -L /workspace/repo-vul/src-vul/zlib -lz 2>&1 && ./test_ktar_asan /workspace/original_testcase.gz 2>&1

message.content:
Now I see the critical issue! Look at the window copy path:

```c
from = window;
if (wnext == 0) {           /* very common case */
    from += wsize - op;
    if (op < len) {         /* some from window */
        len -= op;
        do {
            *out++ = *from++;
        } while (--op);
        from = out - dist;  /* rest from output */
    }
}
```

If `op >= len`, the `if (op < len)` block is skipped. Then `from` still points to the window. Then the unrolled loop runs:

```c
while (len > 2) {
    *out++ = *from++;
    *out++ = *from++;
    *out++ = *from++;
    len -= 3;
}
```

This copies `len` bytes from the window to `out`. But `len` could be up to 258, and `out` could be close to `end`. This is the overflow!

But wait, this is copying from the window, not from the output buffer. The window is 32K bytes, so reading from it is fine. The issue is writing to `out`.

Actually, I think I've been overthinking this. Let me just try to reproduce the crash with the original testcase and the exact same setup as the fuzzer:

